# Assembled by copier from github.com/gitattributes/gitattributes, the
# published collection. The template repository keeps upstream's files
# unedited, fetched by vendir at the commit its vendir.lock.yml records, and
# which of them apply follows the answers in .copier-answers.yml. A rule of
# this repository's own, such as a Git LFS filter, is the gitattributes_own
# answer, rendered at the end, so nothing in this file is written by hand.

# Upstream's Common.gitattributes sets `* text=auto` and leaves the working
# tree's endings to core.autocrlf, which is set per machine. On Windows that
# checks out CRLF, so a formatter or a script that writes one LF line into a
# checked out file makes that file genuinely mixed; the mixed-line-ending hook
# then rewrites the whole file and fails the commit, and the rewrite produces
# no diff because the index normalises either way. Measured at 205 files
# rewritten per run on a Windows checkout of Orderbooks.
#
# Naming eol here settles it in the repository rather than on each machine, and
# it agrees with the hook's --fix=lf. A later line overrides an earlier one
# attribute by attribute, so upstream's `* text=auto` below does not clear this
# eol, and the `*.bat`, `*.cmd`, `*.ps1` and `*.csv` lines that ask for CRLF
# still win for those extensions. Adopting this renormalises a Windows working
# tree once; the index and every other platform are unchanged.
*          text=auto eol=lf

